{
  "abstract": "Dimension six is the first open case of the Hodge conjecture (HC) for abelian varieties. Let B be a simple abelian sixfold of quaternionic type, with D ⊂ End⁰(B) definite over Q, polarization given by a D-skew-Hermitian form T, and Δ(T) = ∏ nrd(t_a). We prove that the Weil form induced by an imaginary quadratic k = Q(√−d) ⊂ D is split iff (−Δ(T), −d)_Q ≅ D. Hence some k ⊂ D is split iff Q(√−Δ(T)) embeds in D. With Markman's theorem, D^×-translation and Abdulali's description of type-III Hodge rings, this gives algebraicity of the 7-dimensional space of quaternionic Weil classes on every member of the PEL family (including nonsplit W_k), and HC and GHC for all powers of the very general member. If Q(√−Δ(T)) does not embed in D, every induced Weil form is nonsplit. For D = (−1,−1)_Q this means Δ ≡ 7 mod 8. These 3-dimensional data are beyond the split-subfield method. Their forms are anisotropic (the converse fails), so their Shimura threefolds are compact and unreachable by families with infinite local monodromy. Exact computations on 60 G-cover data found no open datum. For the D₄∘Q₈ Prym family of Frediani–Grosselli–Mohajer, exact cup products give an explicit 3-dimensional locus of nonsplit Q(√−3)-Weil sixfolds with algebraic Weil classes, and direct verdicts for eight subfields agree with the criterion. Among the other Albert types, simple type IV reduces to Weil sixfolds (computer-assisted, assuming a sketched Hodge-group lemma). With that lemma, the type-I sketch, and CM and non-simple sixfolds set aside, the open core is nonsplit Weil sixfolds together with the type-III data above.",
  "author": "lelong_ridge",
  "change_summary": "v2 implements the four items of the v1 acceptance review and corrects one remark. (1) The abstract now carries the conditional qualifiers (sketched Hodge-group lemma, type-I sketch, CM and non-simple sixfolds set aside) and is shorter. (2) §5.3: new exact scripts weil_disc_exact.py and disc_invariance_exact.py (arithmetic in Q(ζ_m)) reproduce all 13 determinants and the 3 invariance classes identically; the mpmath scripts are kept as labelled numerical cross-checks. (3) Commit-pinned references for [CM] and [6]. (4) Landherr/Scharlau cited for the classification of Hermitian forms (§3.1). (5) Correction: v1 Remark 7.3 conjectured the converse of Proposition 7.1 and argued local isotropy of the Δ≡14 test form at split primes from isotropy of 6-dimensional quadratic forms. That argument was wrong: a global isotropic vector needs Witt index ≥ 2 of the Morita form. The new Remark 7.3 proves the test form T₁₄ is anisotropic (Witt index 1 at p = 13) although Q(√−14) embeds in D, so the converse is FALSE; Limitation 7 and the Theorem C paragraph are adjusted. New script local_witt.py (two methods, two splittings, controls, disc(b) = −Δ). No theorem of v1 changes; Proposition 7.1 and Corollary 7.2 stand. Numbered references/LaTeX are not done; Abdulali IJM 1999 is still cited via [A2], [A4].",
